WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.211
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B two.

2
00:00:05.511 --> 00:00:08.886
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:14.148
Unit seventeen: Noun Clauses. Today you will use whole clauses as nouns.

4
00:00:14.448 --> 00:00:16.961
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.005
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:23.118
That clause.

7
00:00:24.117 --> 00:00:26.569
That clause means a clause beginning with that.

8
00:00:27.183 --> 00:00:28.579
I know that she left.

9
00:00:29.183 --> 00:00:31.894
Your turn. Say: That clause.

10
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:36.106
That clause.

11
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:37.898
What he said.

12
00:00:38.883 --> 00:00:41.254
What he said means a noun clause with what.

13
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.315
What he said surprised me.

14
00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:46.186
Your turn. Say: What he said.

15
00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:50.185
What he said.

16
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:51.719
Whether.

17
00:00:52.717 --> 00:00:55.506
Whether means used for yes/no embedded questions.

18
00:00:56.117 --> 00:00:57.780
I wonder whether she knows.

19
00:00:58.383 --> 00:01:00.664
Your turn. Say: Whether.

20
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:04.211
Whether.

21
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.427
Embedded question.

22
00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:11.309
Embedded question means a question inside another sentence.

23
00:01:11.917 --> 00:01:13.568
Do you know where he lives?

24
00:01:14.183 --> 00:01:17.230
Your turn. Say: Embedded question.

25
00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:21.544
Embedded question.

26
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:23.726
Subject clause.

27
00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:27.436
Subject clause means a noun clause as subject.

28
00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.365
What he said is true.

29
00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.695
Your turn. Say: Subject clause.

30
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:37.115
Subject clause.

31
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.292
Object clause.

32
00:01:40.283 --> 00:01:42.991
Object clause means a noun clause as object.

33
00:01:43.583 --> 00:01:44.875
I know what he said.

34
00:01:45.483 --> 00:01:48.159
Your turn. Say: Object clause.

35
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.640
Object clause.

36
00:01:53.383 --> 00:01:54.640
Complement clause.

37
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.404
Complement clause means a noun clause after be.

38
00:01:59.017 --> 00:02:00.726
The problem is that I'm late.

39
00:02:01.317 --> 00:02:04.573
Your turn. Say: Complement clause.

40
00:02:07.433 --> 00:02:08.910
Complement clause.

41
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:11.115
Nominal clause.

42
00:02:12.117 --> 00:02:14.836
Nominal clause means another name for a noun clause.

43
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:16.962
A nominal clause.

44
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.493
Your turn. Say: Nominal clause.

45
00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:24.776
Nominal clause.

46
00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:29.612
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.367
Visal, complete: What he said was…

48
00:02:34.483 --> 00:02:45.799
What he said was surprising. A noun clause works like a noun, so it can be a subject, an object or a complement. It starts with that, what, whether, if or a question word.

49
00:02:46.883 --> 00:02:48.233
What are the patterns?

50
00:02:49.317 --> 00:03:03.433
Subject: What she wants is clear. Object: I know that you are tired. Complement: The problem is that we have no time. Embedded question: I don't know where he lives, with statement order.

51
00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:05.866
Can we omit that?

52
00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:20.565
After many verbs, yes: I think you are right. After a noun or adjective we usually keep it: the fact that, I'm glad that. Whether is used for choices: I wonder whether it will rain or not.

53
00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:23.746
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.583 --> 00:03:29.590
Grammar focus: Noun clauses: that and what and whether and wh-.

55
00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:30.995
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.683 --> 00:03:33.358
What he said was surprising.

57
00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:35.750
I know that you are tired.

58
00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:38.731
The problem is that we have no time.

59
00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:48.951
A noun clause can be subject, object or complement. Introducers: that, what, whether / if, wh- words. Use statement word order inside the clause.

60
00:03:49.717 --> 00:03:53.286
Be careful! Don't say: I know where does he live.

61
00:03:55.233 --> 00:03:57.535
Say: I know where he lives.

62
00:03:57.835 --> 00:03:59.254
Use statement order.

63
00:04:00.067 --> 00:04:02.696
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:04.989
Clauses as Nouns.

65
00:04:05.289 --> 00:04:07.300
What he said at the meeting was surprising.

66
00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.334
I know that the exam is difficult.

67
00:04:11.333 --> 00:04:13.159
The problem is that we have no time.

68
00:04:14.167 --> 00:04:15.899
I wonder whether she will come.

69
00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:18.447
Where he lives is a mystery.

70
00:04:19.533 --> 00:04:23.361
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:24.217 --> 00:04:25.926
blank she wants is clear.

72
00:04:26.226 --> 00:04:29.090
A, What. B, That. C, Whether.

73
00:04:32.633 --> 00:04:34.285
The answer is A: What.

74
00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.124
I wonder blank it will rain.

75
00:04:37.424 --> 00:04:40.822
A, what. B, who. C, whether.

76
00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:46.192
The answer is C: whether.

77
00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.164
The fact blank he left worries me.

78
00:04:49.464 --> 00:04:52.397
A, that. B, what. C, which.

79
00:04:55.933 --> 00:04:57.840
The answer is A: that.

80
00:04:58.983 --> 00:05:00.518
Which uses statement order?

81
00:05:00.818 --> 00:05:06.515
A, I know where does he live.. B, I know where he lives.. C, I know where lives he..

82
00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:12.751
The answer is B: I know where he lives..

83
00:05:13.917 --> 00:05:16.508
According to the reading, what is the problem?

84
00:05:16.808 --> 00:05:20.752
A, no time. B, no money. C, no food.

85
00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:27.008
The answer is A: no time.

86
00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.572
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:32.417 --> 00:05:35.612
Write a sentence that begins with a noun clause as the subject.

88
00:05:44.417 --> 00:05:45.789
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:46.089 --> 00:05:55.884
What she said at the meeting changed my mind. Whether we should continue is still an open question. A noun clause as the subject works like a noun.

90
00:05:56.184 --> 00:05:57.859
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:04.862
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and noun clauses: that and what and whether and wh-.

92
00:06:05.162 --> 00:06:10.090
Today's value is intellectual rigor. Hold your ideas to the highest standard of evidence.

93
00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:13.193
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:13.493 --> 00:06:16.552
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

